  "rawCandidate": "```json\n{\"schemaVersion\":1,\"selectedContextPaths\":[\"README.md\",\"checks/test_parser.py\",\"src/parser.py\"],\"plan\":{\"mutationTargets\":[],\"requestedCheckIds\":[]},\"answer\":{\"kind\":\"read_only\",\"files\":[],\"citations\":[\"README.md\",\"checks/test_parser.py\",\"src/parser.py\"],\"findingIds\":[],\"summary\":\"The parser returns bool(value), and Python treats any non-empty string as truthy, so bool('false') evaluates to True. The fixture asserts parse_enabled('false') is False (checks/test_parser.py), which fails because the implementation never inspects the literal contents of the string—it only tests for emptiness. The README contract (README.md) requires the parser to recognize explicit 'true' and 'false' values, but the current implementation maps every non-empty token to True. No edit is proposed; the false case fails purely because bool() cannot distinguish the literal token 'false' from any other non-empty string.\"}}\n```",
